WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.267
One of the best uses of unsafe Rust is to
implement an abstraction that callers can

2
00:00:04.347 --> 00:00:04.901
use safely.

3
00:00:04.981 --> 00:00:07.086
The unsafe code may be necessary
internally,

4
00:00:07.166 --> 00:00:10.324
but the public API should make invalid use
difficult or impossible.

5
00:00:10.404 --> 00:00:14.171
That is the difference between unsafe as a
controlled implementation detail and

6
00:00:14.251 --> 00:00:16.301
unsafe as a burden pushed onto every
caller.

7
00:00:16.381 --> 00:00:18.153
Encapsulation protects invariants.

8
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:21.723
If a type depends on a buffer being
initialized, a pointer remaining valid,

9
00:00:21.803 --> 00:00:24.186
or a state transition happening in a
certain order,

10
00:00:24.266 --> 00:00:26.648
the public interface should preserve those
rules.

11
00:00:26.728 --> 00:00:30.218
Limited visibility, constructor
validation, ownership-aware methods,

12
00:00:30.298 --> 00:00:33.677
and explicit lifetime relationships can
help keep the contract intact.

13
00:00:33.757 --> 00:00:36.970
Type-state patterns can be useful when a
value moves through phases.

14
00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.097
Instead of storing a flag that callers
must interpret correctly,

15
00:00:40.177 --> 00:00:42.171
the API can represent the phase in the
type.

16
00:00:42.251 --> 00:00:45.908
That can prevent operations from being
called before initialization or after

17
00:00:45.988 --> 00:00:46.431
closure.

18
00:00:46.511 --> 00:00:49.447
The goal is to let the compiler enforce
more of the safety story.

19
00:00:49.527 --> 00:00:52.962
A safe wrapper is only valid if it
prevents safe callers from violating the

20
00:00:53.042 --> 00:00:55.258
assumptions required by the unsafe
internals.

21
00:00:55.338 --> 00:00:59.160
Review should ask whether ordinary safe
code can accidentally produce an invalid

22
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:02.841
state, create aliasing that the internals
cannot handle, misuse a lifetime,

23
00:01:02.921 --> 00:01:05.081
or bypass validation through a public
method.

24
00:01:05.161 --> 00:01:08.596
The review question is not only whether
the unsafe block is correct today.

25
00:01:08.676 --> 00:01:12.665
It is also whether tomorrow's maintainer
can change nearby code without breaking the

26
00:01:12.745 --> 00:01:13.243
contract.

27
00:01:13.323 --> 00:01:16.813
Good abstractions make the unsafe
assumption local, documented, tested,

28
00:01:16.893 --> 00:01:18.500
and hard to violate accidentally.
